{"groups":[{"members":["c1"],"primary":"c1","title":"California Tenant Protection Act: just cause required to terminate tenancies","requirement":"Once a tenant has continuously and lawfully occupied a unit for 12 months, or 24 months if an adult tenant was added before an existing tenant reached 24 months, the owner may terminate the tenancy only for an at-fault or no-fault just cause stated in the written termination notice. For no-fault terminations, the owner must either pay relocation assistance equal to one month's rent within 15 calendar days of serving the notice or waive the final month's rent.","key_value":"Just cause required after 12 months of occupancy (24 months if adult tenants were added); no-fault relocation assistance of one month's rent, paid within 15 calendar days of service of the notice, or waiver of the final month's rent","status":"in_force","effective_date":"2024-04-01","end_date":"2030-01-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1946.2","coverage_conditions":"Residential real property whose tenants have continuously and lawfully occupied it for the required period. The section does not apply to property covered by a local just cause ordinance adopted on or before September 1, 2019, or adopted or amended later if that ordinance is more protective. A property is not subject to both.","exemptions":"Transient and tourist hotels; nonprofit hospitals, religious facilities, extended care facilities, licensed residential care facilities for the elderly, and adult residential facilities; school or university dormitories; tenants who share a bathroom or kitchen with an owner-occupant; owner-occupied single-family residences renting no more than two units or bedrooms; owner-occupied duplexes where the owner lived in one unit at the start of the tenancy; housing with a certificate of occupancy issued within the previous 15 years (except mobilehomes); separately alienable property such as single-family homes or condominiums owned by natural persons, subject to the statute's conditions.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c2"],"primary":"c2","title":"Housing Crisis Act: relocation for lower-income tenants displaced by demolition","requirement":"Lower-income households (80% AMI or lower) displaced when Protected Units are demolished for new construction must receive relocation benefits consistent with Gov. Code § 7260 et seq. The landlord must pay the higher of the State Housing Crisis Act amount or the City amount.","key_value":null,"status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Gov. Code § 66300 et seq.","coverage_conditions":"Lower-income households displaced by demolition of Protected Units for new construction.","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c2","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c4","c3"],"primary":"c4","title":"California relocation payments for temporary capital improvement displacement of less than 20 days","requirement":"When a tenant household is temporarily displaced for less than 20 days for capital improvement work, the owner must pay a per-day relocation amount plus actual moving expenses. These payments are governed by Civ. Code § 1947.9, not by San Francisco Rent Ordinance § 37.9C.","key_value":"$446.00/day plus actual moving expenses","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1947.9","coverage_conditions":"Temporary capital improvement displacement lasting less than 20 days.","exemptions":null,"coverage_from":"c4","conflict_note":"D083 (c4) lists two per-day amounts, $446.00/day and $436.00/day, without labeling their effective periods. The $446.00 figure is kept as the likely current amount, but this cannot be confirmed from the document. D082 (c3) gives no amount."}],"dropped":[]}
